  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry today, then expect increasing scattered \nafternoon/evening showers/storms beginning Thursday and \ncontinuing through early next week.\n\nLow stratus and fog across the mountains this morning will\ncontinue to increase in coverage through daybreak. Will likely\nhave areas of dense fog lingering for at least an hour or two\nafter sunrise before dissipating. Expecting another dry day\ntoday with high pressure remaining in control. \n\nThe upper ridge is currently centered over the TN valley, but is \nforecast to retrograde farther west and become centered over \nthe Arl-La- Tex by the weekend. This will allow a front over \nthe Great Lakes to sag south over the Mid- Atlantic with \nincreasing moisture leading to a daily chance for showers and a \nfew storms beginning Thursday and persisting through early next \nweek. Overall, severe threat remains low given lack of shear to \nsupport organized deep convection. \n\nWarmest temperatures will occur Thursday and Friday, featuring\nwidespread upper 80s to mid 90s. Will see temperatures lower \nsomewhat again over the weekend and early next week as the \nridging shifts west, but will still be above normal for mid\nSeptember.",
